Full license texts for the bundled SDK libraries live alongside their source
under packages/romdev/src/platforms/*/lib/. Emulator-core and compiler license
texts travel with their upstream source, which is fetched at build time from
the pinned commits recorded in packages/romdev/scripts/versions.json.


Bundled data  (cheat database - used by gameCheats / applyCheat / makeCheat)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  romdev bundles a cheat database under packages/romdev/src/cheats/index/ - a
  pre-parsed, per-platform index built from the community-aggregated RetroArch /
  libretro-database cheat collection (the same data RetroArch, RetroDECK, and
  Batocera ship). These cheat codes are an aggregation assembled over years from
  Game Genie / Pro Action Replay code books and community cheat dumps; the
  collection has no single uniform license and individual codes carry no stated
  provenance. romdev redistributes a pre-parsed form (game name → decoded
  address/value entries) rather than the raw .cht files, and uses it only as a
  read/inspect aid + non-destructive cheat application. It is not linked into any
  built game and does not affect the license of games you make. Source:
  https://github.com/libretro/libretro-database (cht/). Rebuild the index from a
  local .cht tree with packages/romdev/scripts/build-cheat-index.mjs.


Bundled data  (reference-core-derived recompiler data)
------------------------------------------------------
  src/analysis/recompile/wat/genesis/gpgx-derived.json.gz holds the parsed
  ASTs of genesis_plus_gx's Musashi 68000 opcode handlers and MAME z80.c
  handlers plus gpgx's cycle/HV tables, generated from the pinned, patched
  gpgx tree by scripts/recompile-gen/genesis-gpgx.mjs (the file records the
  upstream commit, the patch and a sha256 of every source file it read), and
  src/analysis/recompile/wat/genesis/runtime.js is a port of gpgx's VDP,
  memory map and frame loop. Both are under the Genesis Plus GX license
  (NON-COMMERCIAL, see above; z80.c additionally carries Juergen
  Buchmueller's non-commercial notice). Unlike an emulator core, this code
  IS copied into the output: a wasmcart built by
  disasm({target:'recompile', emit:'wasmcart', platform:'genesis'}) contains
  code transpiled from gpgx and may not be sold or used commercially.

  src/analysis/recompile/wat/sms/gpgx-sound.json (blip_buf.c's bl_step,
  psg.c's volume table, ym2612.c's tables, generated by
  scripts/recompile-gen/gpgx-sound.mjs with the source's sha256) and the
  sound ports src/analysis/recompile/wat/sms/psg.js (SN76489 + blip_buf,
  Blip_Buffer by Shay Green is LGPL-2.1, as modified by gpgx) and
  src/analysis/recompile/wat/genesis/ym2612.js (the MAME-derived YM2612 as
  gpgx ships it) are likewise under the Genesis Plus GX license
  (NON-COMMERCIAL). SMS / Game Gear and Genesis wasmcarts built with sound
  (the default) contain code ported from them.

  src/analysis/recompile/wat/genesis/gpgx-svp.json is genesis_plus_gx's SVP
  chip (core/cart_hw/svp/svp.c + ssp16.c: the SSP1601 DSP interpreter and the
  SVP memory controller, (c) 2008 Grazvydas "notaz" Ignotas, adapted by
  Eke-Eke) compiled to WebAssembly and printed as WAT by
  scripts/recompile-gen/gpgx-svp.mjs (the file records the upstream commit,
  the compiler and a sha256 of every source file). Its notice reads "Free for
  non-commercial use. For commercial use, separate licencing terms must be
  obtained." A Genesis wasmcart built from an SVP cart contains that code and
  is NON-COMMERCIAL on both counts.

m2c (decompiler) - romdevtools depends on the separate romdev-m2c package,
a JavaScript port of m2c (https://github.com/matt-kempster/m2c) licensed
GPL-3.0, with its own LICENSE and NOTICE; its C parser is the separate
romdev-pycparser package (BSD-3). romdevtools only invokes it; the pseudocode it
generates for the user's project is a draft of the user's own work.
